.TH PAGE_OWNER_SORT 1 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
page_owner_sort \- combine and sort the records of a kernel page owner report
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B \%page_owner_sort
.RB [ \-a | \-m | \-n | \-p | \-P | \-s | \-t ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-\-pid
.IR pidlist ]
.RB [ \-\-tgid
.IR tgidlist ]
.RB [ \-\-name
.IR namelist ]
.RB [ \-\-cull
.IR rules ]
.RB [ \-\-sort
.IR order ]
.I input
.I output
.br
.B \%page_owner_sort \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B \%page_owner_sort
reads a saved copy of the kernel's page owner report, which records the
allocation of every allocated page, combines identical records, and
writes them to
.I output
sorted by one key.
.PP
The input consists of records separated by empty lines; a record at the
end of the input that is not followed by an empty line is ignored.
In the kernel's report, each record starts with a line of the form
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
Page allocated via order \fIO\fP, mask ..., pid \fIP\fP, tgid \fIT\fP (\fIname\fP), ts \fIS\fP ns
.EE
.in
.PP
followed by a line starting with
.B PFN
and by the stack trace of the allocation.
From each record,
.B \%page_owner_sort
takes the allocation order (a record counts as 2 to the power
.I O
pages), the process ID, the thread group ID, the task command name, the
timestamp, and the stack trace, which is the text after the first line.
Lines starting with
.B PFN
are removed from the record text.
Each record is also classified by allocator:
.B CMA
or
.B SLAB
if its
.B PFN
line contains
.B CMA
or
.B slab
respectively,
.B VMALLOC
if the stack trace line before
.B \%__vmalloc_node_range
contains
.BR \%alloc_pages ,
and
.B OTHERS
if none of these applies.
.PP
By default, records whose text is identical are combined into one, with
a count of the times it was seen and the total number of pages.
Unless
.B \-a
is given, the
.RI \(dq "ts " S " ns" \(dq
timestamp is removed from the record text first, so that records that
differ only in their timestamp are combined.
With
.BR \-\-cull ,
records are combined when they agree in the fields named in
.I rules
instead.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-cull ,
each combined record is written as a line of the form
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
\fIN\fP times, \fIM\fP pages, allocated by \fIALLOCATOR\fP :
.EE
.in
.PP
followed by the record text and an empty line.
With
.BR \-\-cull ,
each combined record is written as a line that starts with
.RI \(dq N " times, " M " pages" \(dq
and continues with these items where they apply:
.IP \(bu 3
.RI "\(dq, PID " pid \(dq,
when culling by pid or selecting with
.BR \-\-pid ;
.IP \(bu 3
.RI "\(dq, TGID " tgid \(dq,
when culling by tgid or selecting with
.BR \-\-tgid ;
.IP \(bu 3
.RI "\(dq, \%task_comm_name: " name \(dq,
when culling by name or selecting with
.BR \-\-name ;
.IP \(bu 3
.RI "\(dq, allocated by " ALLOCATOR \(dq,
when culling by allocator;
.IP \(bu 3
a colon, followed on the next lines by the stack trace, when culling by
stack trace.
.PP
Progress messages are printed to standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
At most one of
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-P ,
.B \-s
and
.B \-t
may be given; it selects the sort order of the output.
.TP
.B \-a
Sort by allocation timestamp, oldest first.
Timestamps are kept in the record text, so records are combined only if
their timestamps are equal.
.TP
.B \-m
Sort by total number of pages, largest first.
.TP
.B \-n
Sort by task command name.
.TP
.B \-p
Sort by process ID, smallest first.
.TP
.B \-P
Sort by thread group ID, smallest first.
.TP
.B \-s
Sort by stack trace.
.TP
.B \-t
Sort by the number of times a record was seen, largest first.
This is the default.
.TP
.B \-d
Print a message to standard error for each field that cannot be found or
parsed in a record.
.TP
.BI \-\-pid " pidlist"
Select only records whose process ID is in
.IR pidlist ,
a comma-separated list of decimal numbers.
.TP
.BI \-\-tgid " tgidlist"
Select only records whose thread group ID is in
.IR tgidlist ,
a comma-separated list of decimal numbers.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " namelist"
Select only records whose task command name is exactly one of the names
in the comma-separated list
.IR namelist .
.IP
When more than one of
.BR \-\-pid ,
.B \-\-tgid
and
.B \-\-name
is given, a record must match all of them.
.TP
.BI \-\-cull " rules"
Combine records that agree in the fields listed in
.IR rules ,
a comma-separated list of these keys:
.BR pid " or " p ,
.BR tgid " or " tg ,
.BR name " or " n ,
.BR stacktrace " or " st ,
and
.BR allocator " or " ator .
.TP
.BI \-\-sort " order"
Parse a sort order given as a comma-separated list of keys, each
optionally preceded by
.B +
(ascending, the default) or
.B \-
(descending).
The keys are those of
.B \-\-cull
and also
.BR \%alloc_ts " or " at
(allocation timestamp) and
.BR txt " or " T
(the full record text).
An unknown key is an error.
In this version the order given with
.B \-\-sort
is replaced by the single-key order selected by the options above (by
default that of
.BR \-t ),
so it has no effect on the output.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage message and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B \%page_owner_sort
exits with status 1 if an option or its argument is invalid, if more than
one sort option is given, if
.I input
or
.I output
is missing, or if either file cannot be opened.
Otherwise it exits with status 0, even when it stops early because the
input holds more records than it can store or memory runs out; the output
is then empty.
.SH NOTES
The kernel writes the page owner report to
.I \%/sys/kernel/debug/page_owner
in debugfs.
The kernel must be built with
.B \%CONFIG_PAGE_OWNER
and booted with
.BR \%page_owner=on .
Reading the report normally requires privilege;
.B \%page_owner_sort
itself needs none.
.PP
.B \%page_owner_sort
reserves space for one record for every 100 bytes of the size of
.IR input ,
so
.I input
must be a regular file; when it reads from a pipe it stops with the
message
.RB \(dq "\%max_size too small??" \(dq.
.PP
The page owner report and this program are described in this file in
the Linux source:
.RS
.I \%Documentation/mm/page_owner.rst
.RE
.PP
That document also describes the options
.B \-f
and
.B \-r
and the keys
.BR f ,
.BR free ,
.B ft
and
.BR \%free_ts ,
which this version does not implement.
.SH EXAMPLES
Save the report and sort it by total number of pages:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
cat /sys/kernel/debug/page_owner > page_owner_full.txt
page_owner_sort \-m page_owner_full.txt sorted_page_owner.txt
.EE
.in
.PP
Combine the records by stack trace, process ID and command name:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
page_owner_sort \-\-cull=st,pid,name page_owner_full.txt culled.txt
.EE
.in
